      "answer": "The customer whose issue started after the 2026-02-20 taxonomy rollout is BlueHarbor Logistics. Their problem was that Event Nexus search relevance degraded, with saved searches returning poor and duplicate results, increasing manual triage time by about 6 hours per day. This was traced to the index weighting still referencing old schema fields and mismatches in the schema registry versions after the taxonomy change on 2026-02-20 [art_8b0063fbb3cb, art_0bccc580184e, art_bd3560dfe194].\n\nThe proof plan proposed to get BlueHarbor comfortable with renewal is a 7-10 business day proof involving:\n\n- Updating the index weighting and adding a mapping layer that translates the new taxonomy terms to the old query terms for the top 20 saved searches.\n\n- Running an A/B test in staging starting around 2026-03-22, lasting 5 business days for tuning plus 2 business days for validation.\n\n- Validation criteria include achieving a top-5 correct hit rate of at least 80% for prioritized queries, no regression in EN-RULES-ENGINE suppression logic, and incident correlation latency under 200ms.\n\n- If validation passes, a 1-day production rollout with 72 hours of monitoring.\n\nCommercial terms include a 90-day professional services engagement at a fixed price of $48,000, with a 30% discount if BlueHarbor concurrently signs for Orchestrator seat expansion. There is also a conditional credit clause: if the proof does not meet acceptance criteria within 10 business days, BlueHarbor will receive a one-quarter invoice credit prorated against the renewal for the upcoming year [art_0bccc580184e, art_bd3560dfe194, art_c9970c1dc932].\n\nThis plan aims to demonstrate measurable improvement quickly to satisfy BlueHarbor's executive mandate to reduce manual triage by 40% and to secure renewal under acceptable risk terms.",
